<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<templates id="template" xml:space="preserve">

    <t t-name="hlv_pos_theme.ProductsWidget" t-inherit="point_of_sale.ProductsWidget" t-inherit-mode="extension" owl="1">
        <!-- 
            Strategy: 
            Replace the default structure to wrap everything in a 2-column container.
            We unfortunately can't drastically re-arrange WITHOUT replacing the main logic often,
            BUT Odoo 16/17/18 tries to be modular.
            
            By default ProductsWidget has:
            <div class="products-widget ...">
               <ProductsWidgetControlPanel ... />
               <div class="product-list-container ...">
                   ...
               </div>
            </div>

            ProductsWidgetControlPanel is where Categories usually live (breadcrumbs/selector).
            OR in older versions it was separate.
            
            Let's target the root div and try to enforce a split via CSS mainly, 
            but if we can separate the "search" from "categories", that is better.
            
            Actually, the safest sidebar approach in recent Odoo is manipulating CSS Grid/Flex on the main container
            AND moving the "Category List" logic.
            
            However, since I don't know the EXACT internal template of standard Odoo 18 without reading it, 
            I will try a CSS-Heavy approach first where we force the Category container to be left aligned, 
            or I inject a specific sidebar container if possible.
            
            Wait, standard Odoo puts categories INSIDE the Product List area sometimes or above it.
            
            Let's try to RE-ORGANIZE the top level structure.
        -->
        
        <!-- 
             Investigating Odoo 18, `ProductsWidget` usually separates `ProductsWidgetControlPanel` (search + cats) 
             and `ProductList`.
             
             We will HIDE the default category display in ControlPanel (via CSS or inheritance removal)
             and INSERT a new Sidebar component or just re-style the existing list if it's rendered there.
             
             Refining plan: 
             If we simply float the category-list to the left and the products to the right, we can achieve this.
             
             Let's wrap the contents in a flex container.
        -->
        
        <xpath expr="//div[hasclass('products-widget')]" position="attributes">
            <attribute name="class">products-widget hlv-sidebar-layout</attribute>
        </xpath>

    </t>

</templates>
